== 4. Eight solutions of phi(x) = 36
PASS  phi(x) >= sqrt(x/2) on the sieve range
PASS  phi(x) = 36: [37, 57, 63, 74, 76, 108, 114, 126]
PASS  36 is the least n with exactly eight solutions (multiplicities below 36: 1:2, 2:3, 4:4, 6:4, 8:5, 10:2, 12:6, 16:6, 18:4, 20:5, 22:2, 24:10, 28:2, 30:2, 32:7)
PASS  7 = 2*3 + 1, 19 = 2*3^2 + 1, 37 = 2^2 3^2 + 1: lattice points (1,1), (1,2), (2,2)
PASS    37 = 37  ->  (2, 2) = (2,2)
PASS    57 = 3^1 * 19  ->  (1, 0) + (1, 2) = (2,2)
PASS    63 = 3^2 * 7  ->  (1, 1) + (1, 1) = (2,2)
PASS    74 = 2^1 * 37  ->  (0, 0) + (2, 2) = (2,2)
PASS    76 = 2^2 * 19  ->  (1, 0) + (1, 2) = (2,2)
PASS   108 = 2^2 * 3^3  ->  (1, 0) + (1, 2) = (2,2)
PASS   114 = 2^1 * 3^1 * 19  ->  (0, 0) + (1, 0) + (1, 2) = (2,2)
PASS   126 = 2^1 * 3^2 * 7  ->  (0, 0) + (1, 1) + (1, 1) = (2,2)
PASS  8 = 1 (no prime: 108) + 2 (with 7) + 3 (with 19) + 2 (with 37)
PASS  degree of cos(2 pi/n) is phi(n)/2 = 18 = 2 * 3^2 for the eight n; phi(10) = 4 = 2^2
PASS  cos(2 pi/10) = (1 + sqrt 5)/4

== 5. F(n) = sum over d | n of tau(d)
PASS  F from the definition = product of T_(a+1), n <= 5000
PASS  orbit 72 -> 60 -> 54 -> 30 -> 27 -> 10 -> 9 -> 6 -> 9
PASS  fixed points up to 10^6: 1, 3, 18, 36
PASS  F(n) < n outside {1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 18, 24, 36}, n <= 10^6
PASS  2-cycle 6 <-> 9
PASS  the nine divisors of 36 have 36 divisors in all: F(36) = 36
PASS  F(n) = 3n/2 exactly at n = 2, 4, 6, 12 (n <= 10^6)
PASS  every orbit from n <= 10^6 ends at 1, 3, 18, 36 or {6,9}: {1: 1, 3: 78498, '{6,9}': 779257, 18: 140650, 36: 1594}
PASS  tau_k(p^2 q^2) = T_k^2 = 1^3 + ... + k^3 for k < 200
PASS  T_k^2 fixed by tau_k iff T_k = p*q: k = [3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 13, 22, 37, 46, 58] (k <= 60); fixed points 36, 100, 225, 441

